Below are the changes for the current release.
See the CHANGES file for changes in older releases.
See the RELEASENOTES file for a summary of changes in each release.
Issue # numbers mentioned below can be found on Github. For more details, add
the issue number to the end of the URL: https://github.com/swig/swig/issues/

Version 4.6.0 (in progress)
===========================

2026-10-01: olly
	    [Guile] Typemaps handling std::string no longer truncate strings at
	    an embedded null character.

2026-10-01: olly
	    [Guile] Remove non-functional `cplusplus.i` from library.  This defines
	    typemaps with a language name, which we stopped supporting in SWIG 4.1.0
	    after deprecating it about 20 years ago.

	    `cplusplus.i` defined some typemaps for std::string (use
	    `std_string.i` instead) and some for std::complex which I've moved
	    to new library files `complex.i` and `std_complex.i`.

2026-09-20: wsfulton
            [Guile] An array of char is now wrapped as a string, as it is in
            every other target language, rather than as an opaque pointer:

              char letters[4] = "abc";

            A function parameter of type 'char [N]' or 'char []' now likewise
            requires a string and no longer accepts a pointer.

            *** POTENTIAL INCOMPATIBILITY ***

2026-09-19: wsfulton
            A typemap written with an [ANY] array dimension is now matched
            behind a pointer to an array, and can declare local variables, in
            the same way as one behind a reference to an array:

              %typemap(in) int (*)[ANY] (int temp[$1_dim0]) { ... }

              void grid(int (*x)[10]);   // now uses the typemap above

            How a pointer to an array is wrapped without such a typemap is unchanged.

2026-09-19: wsfulton
            [Javascript, Octave, Perl, Python, R, Ruby, Scilab, Tcl] A variable
            or member that is a reference to an array now reads as the array it
            refers to, so a reference to an array of char reads as a string
            instead of as an opaque pointer. A function parameter of type
            'char (&)[N]' now requires a string, as one of type
            'const char (&)[N]' does, and no longer accepts a pointer to the
            array:

              char letters[4] = "abc";
              char (&letters_ref)[4] = letters;   // reads as "abc"
              int count(char (&text)[8]);         // count("abc")

            *** POTENTIAL INCOMPATIBILITY ***

2026-09-19: wsfulton
            A variable or member that is a reference to an array is now set by
            copying the elements of the array it refers to, as the array itself
            is:

              int numbers[4] = {1, 2, 3, 4};
              int (&numbers_ref)[4] = numbers;
              const char letters[4] = "abc";
              const char (&letters_ref)[4] = letters;

            The setter used to assign to the array, which does not compile. A
            reference to an array of const, such as letters_ref, is read only in
            the same way the array itself is.

2026-09-19: wsfulton
            [Javascript, Octave, Perl, Python, R, Ruby, Scilab, Tcl] A function
            parameter of type 'const char (&)[N]' now requires a string and no
            longer accepts the pointer to the array it used to take, as the
            typemaps for it in Lib/typemaps/strings.swg are now used:

              int count(const char (&text)[8]);   // count("abc")

            *** POTENTIAL INCOMPATIBILITY ***

2026-09-19: wsfulton
            A typemap for a reference to an array can now declare local
            variables:

              %typemap(in) const char (&)[ANY] (char temp[$1_dim0]) { ... }

            The locals used to be parsed as part of the pattern, registering
            the typemap as a reference to an array of functions, so it was
            silently never used. The typemaps of this shape in
            Lib/typemaps/strings.swg have therefore never been used until now.

2026-09-19: wsfulton
            A typemap written with an [ANY] array dimension is now matched
            behind a reference to an array as well as on an array:

              %typemap(in) int (&)[ANY] { ... }

              void grid(int (&x)[10]);   // now uses the typemap above

            The dimensions substituted into $1_dim0 and $1_size are those of
            the array the reference refers to. Such a typemap was previously
            never matched, the search going from the type as written straight
            to the SWIGTYPE defaults.

2026-09-12: wsfulton
            New warning 333 replaces warning 302 for two declarations that
            differ only by a constraint:

              template<typename T>
              struct Box {
                Box(T v) requires std::integral<T>;
                Box(T v) requires std::floating_point<T>;
              };

              example.i:4: Warning 333: Declaration of 'Box' as Box::Box(T)
              differing only by a constraint is ignored,
              example.i:3: Warning 333: previous declaration of 'Box' as
              Box::Box(T) is used instead.

            These are distinct C++ overloads rather than a redefinition, so
            warning 302 (Redefinition of identifier 'Box' ignored) was
            misleading. SWIG does not evaluate a constraint, so it still keeps
            only the first declaration; the generated wrapper names the member,
            leaving the C++ compiler to select the overload each instantiation
            satisfies.

2026-09-12: wsfulton
            %template now instantiates every overload of a function template it
            matches, including one whose declaration was renamed:

              %rename(spinner_two) spinner(T, T);

              template<typename T> int spinner(T a);
              template<typename T> int spinner(T a, T b);

              %template(spinner) spinner<int>;

            The renamed declaration used to be skipped, quietly leaving the two
            argument overload unwrapped.

2026-09-12: wsfulton
            A cv-qualifier written on an auto declaration is no longer added to
            one already deduced from the initialiser:

              const int cint = 7;
              const auto &cref = cint;   // const int &
              const auto *cptr = &cint;  // const int *

            The deduced types used to carry the qualifier twice, which showed up
            in the type names quoted by the generated runtime messages, such as
            "in variable 'cptr' of type 'int const const *'".

2026-09-12: wsfulton
            A C++23 explicit object parameter written on any parameter but the
            first is now reported for what it is:

              struct S {
                int m(int a, this S& self);
              };

              example.i:2: Error: The explicit object parameter 'this' must be
              the first parameter of a member function.

            It used to be a bare syntax error.

2026-09-12: wsfulton
            [C] #3169 An rvalue reference is now wrapped like an lvalue
            reference instead of generating code that does not compile:

              int &&rref = 42;

              SWIGIMPORT void rref_set(int * carg1);
              SWIGIMPORT int * rref_get();

            The accessors used to be declared as taking and returning
            'int &&**', and a global rvalue reference variable was written into
            the generated header as a C++ declaration.

2026-09-12: wsfulton
            [Octave] A parameter defaulting to a string or character literal is
            now quoted in the documentation generated by the autodoc feature:

              @deftypefn {Loadable Function} @var{retval} = f (@var{s} = "hi")

            Previously the quotes were dropped, so the documented default was
            not something an Octave user could type. Embedded quotes and
            backslashes are escaped.

2026-09-12: wsfulton
            Fix a crash on input where a class body or a decltype operand is
            never closed, such as:

              %inline %{
              int g = 1;
              decltype(g x;
              %}

            The missing bracket is now reported as an error, Missing ')'.
            Reached end of input.

2026-09-12: wsfulton
            A requires-clause is now part of a function template's signature, so
            two function templates differing only by their constraint are kept
            as distinct overloads:

              template<typename T> requires std::integral<T>       T process(T x);
              template<typename T> requires std::floating_point<T> T process(T x);

            The second declaration used to be dropped with warning 302,
            Redefinition of identifier 'process' ignored, which then let a
            %template instantiate the surviving one whatever type it was given,
            generating a call whose constraint the argument does not satisfy.
            A %template matching both overloads is reported as an ambiguous
            instantiation, SWIG not evaluating constraints. Constructors are
            unaffected and only the first is kept, reported with warning 333.

2026-09-12: wsfulton
            A C++20 abbreviated function template that is %ignored no longer
            hides the other overloads of the same name from %template.  The
            type-constraint is part of the declarator for these, so it is the
            spelling %ignore matches on:

              %ignore classify(std::integral auto);

              int    classify(std::integral auto value);
              double classify(std::floating_point auto value);

              %template(classify_double) classify<double>;

            wraps the std::floating_point overload. An ignored overload that
            was declared first used to hide every other overload of the same
            name, so the %template found only the ignored one and generated
            nothing at all.

2026-09-12: wsfulton
            A C++20 abbreviated function template now instantiates into a
            concrete declarator, so two instantiations wrapped under one name
            are overloads of each other:

              int scaled(auto x) { return int(x * 10); }

              %template(scaled) scaled<int>;
              %template(scaled) scaled<double>;

            wraps both. The declarator kept the 'auto' placeholder through
            instantiation, so the two instantiations had the same signature and
            the second was dropped with warning 302, Redefinition of identifier
            'scaled' ignored.

2026-09-12: wsfulton
            A decltype(auto) variable initialised by a string literal now says
            what stopped it being wrapped:

              decltype(auto) text = "abc";

            reports warning 346, Unable to wrap variable 'text' deduced as a
            reference to an array of characters (ignored), instead of reporting
            that the type could not be deduced from the initialiser. A string
            literal is an lvalue of array type, so the variable is a reference to
            an array of characters, and only whether the literal was wide survives
            into the parse tree, so the character type cannot be recovered. An
            ordinary auto variable is unaffected, deducing the const char * the
            array decays to.

2026-09-12: wsfulton
            A decltype naming a parenthesised variable now uses the type that
            variable was declared with, and names a reference to it where the
            variable is wrapped through a pointer either way:

              int gi = 1;
              int *gp = &gi;
              decltype((gp)) p = gp;  // int *&, was int
              decltype((gi)) i = gi;  // int, unchanged

            The type used to come from the type code of the expression, which
            ignores the declarator, so a variable declared as a pointer, an
            array, a pointer to member or a class type was given the wrong type
            and the generated wrapper did not compile.

            A 'decltype(auto)' variable initialised by a parenthesised variable
            deduces the same type:

              decltype(auto) q = (gp);  // int *&, was int

2026-09-12: wsfulton
            An array initialiser now decays to a pointer when deducing 'auto':

              int arr[4] = {1, 2, 3, 4};
              auto p = arr;   // int *, was int [4]
              auto &r = arr;  // int (&)[4], unchanged

            The variable was wrapped as an array, so its setter copied the
            elements of the assigned array into the pointed-to array instead of
            rebinding the pointer. An 'auto&' variable binds to the array rather
            than deducing from it, so it keeps the array type as before.

2026-09-12: wsfulton
            An 'auto&&' variable is now treated as a forwarding reference, so it
            is an lvalue reference when its initialiser is an lvalue:

              int g = 42;
              auto &&r1 = 42;  // int &&
              auto &&r2 = g;   // int &, was int &&

            SWIG does not track the value category of an arbitrary expression, so
            only an id-expression naming an object, optionally parenthesised, is
            taken to be an lvalue.

2026-09-12: wsfulton
            An 'auto' variable deduced from a reference no longer keeps the
            reference:

              int g = 42;
              int &r = g;
              auto x = r;    // int
              auto &r2 = r;  // int &
              auto *p = &r;  // int *

            An id-expression naming a reference has the type it refers to, so the
            reference is not part of what is deduced from it. Previously the
            reference was kept, giving each of these a doubled reference or a
            pointer to a reference, neither of which is a C++ type, and the
            generated wrapper did not always compile. A decltype naming a
            reference is unchanged, so decltype(r) is still 'int &'.

2026-09-12: wsfulton
            #3484 A director no longer overrides a member function declared final
            when the final declaration overrides a virtual member of a base class:

              struct Base {
                virtual int value(int x);
              };
              struct Derived : Base {
                int value(int x) final;
              };

            The entry the base declaration added to the director vtable was left
            in place, so the generated director class overrode a final member
            function and did not compile. Where it did compile, the director
            method it generated called the base's implementation rather than the
            final one. A final member declared in a class with no base was
            already handled.

2026-09-12: wsfulton
            A declaration copy-list-initialising more than one 'auto' variable now
            reports warning 346 for a braced initialiser list instead of the
            generic unsupported-initialiser one:

              auto first = {1}, second = {2};

            Every declarator shares the one placeholder, so none of them is
            wrapped.

            The warning no longer names the type the list deduces, which is a
            std::initializer_list only when the elements agree on a type and the
            placeholder is 'auto' rather than 'decltype(auto)'. An empty list, a
            list of differing types and a copy-list-initialised 'decltype(auto)'
            variable deduce nothing at all, being ill-formed C++.

2026-09-12: wsfulton
            A decltype(auto) variable is now recognised as initialised by a string
            literal when the whole of the initialiser is one, rather than when the
            text merely starts with one:

              decltype(auto) parens = ("text");   // ignored, a reference to an array
              decltype(auto) pointer = "text" + 1;  // wrapped as const char *

            The first was wrapped as a const char * and its setter did not compile.
            The second is an ordinary expression and is wrapped again.

2026-09-12: wsfulton
            The C++ integral promotion is now applied to 'short' and
            'unsigned short' as well as to the character and boolean types, so an
            'auto' variable deduced from a promoted short is an int:

              short short_value = 1;
              auto promoted = +short_value;  // int, was short

            The variable was previously wrapped as a short, so assigning a value
            outside the range of a short through it failed.

2026-09-12: wsfulton
            A %template naming member function template overloads that differ by
            their cv-qualifier or ref-qualifier is no longer rejected as an
            ambiguous instantiation:

              struct Holder {
                template<typename T> T get(T x) & requires std::integral<T>;
                template<typename T> T get(T x) const & requires std::floating_point<T>;
              };

              %template(get_int) Holder::get<int>;

            Those qualifiers are part of the C++ function signature, so the
            overloads are distinct and no constraint has to be evaluated to
            choose between them.

2026-09-12: wsfulton
            A %template naming two function template overloads whose parameters
            differ only by a top level cv-qualifier is now rejected as an
            ambiguous instantiation:

              int classify(const std::floating_point auto value);
              double classify(std::floating_point auto value);

              // Error: Ambiguous template instantiation of 'classify< double >'.
              %template(classify_double) classify<double>;

            Top level cv-qualification is not part of a C++ function signature,
            so the call is ambiguous in C++ too. SWIG previously reported warning
            509, dropped one overload and wrapped the other, so classify_double
            returned the wrong overload's result.

2026-09-12: wsfulton
            Fix the C++ call generated for an abbreviated function template whose
            'auto' parameter pack is followed by a plain parameter, and the
            partition of the template arguments between two 'auto' parameter
            packs:

              int pack_then_plain(auto... values, int last);
              int two_packs(auto... first, auto... second);

              %template(pack_then_plain_ii) pack_then_plain<int, int>;
              %template(two_packs_ii) two_packs<int, int>;

            The first generated a call with no template arguments at all, which
            did not compile. The second put every argument in the second pack
            rather than one in each.

2026-09-12: wsfulton
            A decltype in a trailing return type naming a parameter declared with
            a function or an array type now gives the pointer type C++ adjusts
            that parameter to:

              auto function_parameter(int fn(int)) -> decltype(fn);  // int (*)(int)
              auto array_parameter(int values[10]) -> decltype(values);  // int *

            The first reported warning 344 and generated a wrapper that did not
            compile.

2026-09-12: wsfulton
            A decltype in a trailing return type naming an 'auto' parameter of an
            abbreviated function template now gives the type the %template
            instantiation supplies for that parameter:

              int value = 2;
              auto shadow(auto value) -> decltype(value) { return value; }

              %template(shadow_double) shadow<double>;

            The return type was previously taken from whatever else the name
            denoted outside the function, with no warning, so shadow_double(2.5)
            returned the integer 2.

2026-09-12: wsfulton
            A structured binding is now parsed and ignored whichever way its
            initialiser is written, and an initialiser containing a semicolon of
            its own no longer takes the rest of the file with it:

              auto [a, b]{point};                    // C++17
              auto [c, d](point);                    // C++20
              auto [e, f] = [] { return make(); }(); // C++17

            The first two were a syntax error and the third reported one after the
            warning, losing everything up to the next semicolon.

2026-09-12: wsfulton
            Fix the C++ call generated for an abbreviated function template whose
            'auto' parameter pack is followed by another 'auto' parameter:

              int pack_then_one(auto... values, auto last);

              %template(pack_then_one_iii) pack_then_one<int, int, int>;

            The generated call named one template argument where it needed one
            for each type the pack takes, and did not compile.

2026-09-12: wsfulton
            A boolean literal is now accepted as a whole constraint:

              template<typename T> T f(T x) requires true;

            This was a syntax error.

2026-09-12: wsfulton
            An alias declaration for a function type written with the alternate
            function syntax now accepts a C++17 noexcept specifier:

              using Function = auto() noexcept -> int;
              using Pointer = auto (*)() noexcept(true) -> int;

            These were a syntax error.

2026-09-12: wsfulton
            Fix parsing of a conversion function to a user defined type whose name
            ends in "decltype":

              struct mydecltype {};
              struct S { operator mydecltype() const; };

            The parenthesis after the type name was taken for the start of a
            decltype operand and consumed into the function name, so a %rename or
            %warnfilter naming the conversion function no longer matched it and
            warning 503 was reported instead.

2026-09-12: wsfulton
            A unary '+' or '-' in the braced initialiser of an 'auto' variable now
            applies the C++ integral promotion when deducing the type:

              auto promoted{+'a'};

            'promoted' is now an 'int' as C++ says. It used to be wrapped as a
            'char', with no warning.

2026-09-12: wsfulton
            A %template naming overloaded function templates that instantiate to
            the same function signature and are told apart only by their
            constraints is now rejected as ambiguous:

              int classify(std::integral auto value);
              double classify(std::floating_point auto value);

              %template(classify_double) classify<double>;

              Error: Ambiguous template instantiation of 'classify< double >'.
              Overloaded declarations of 'classify' with 'std::integral' and
              'std::floating_point' instantiate to the same function signature
              and SWIG does not evaluate constraints to choose between them.

            C++ chooses one of the overloads by constraint satisfaction, which
            SWIG does not evaluate. SWIG used to wrap both overloads, and the
            generated dispatcher could call one of them while converting the
            result to the other one's return type, silently turning a returned
            2.5 into 2.

2026-09-12: wsfulton
            Copy-list-initialisation of an 'auto' variable is now reported for
            what it is:

              auto values = {1, 2};

              Warning 346: Unable to deduce auto type for variable 'values'
              from a braced initialiser list (ignored).

            The list deduces a std::initializer_list, which SWIG declares only
            as a stub that warns rather than wrapping, so the variable stays
            ignored. The declaration used to be reported as an unsupported
            initialiser instead. Note that the direct-list form 'auto value{1};'
            deduces the type of the single element and is wrapped as before.

2026-09-12: wsfulton
            Parentheses around the initialiser of an 'auto' variable no longer
            change the type deduced for it:

              int *pointer;
              auto copy = (pointer);

            'copy' is now an 'int *' as C++ says. It used to be wrapped as an
            'int', with no warning and a wrapper that did not compile.

            The name of a function is no longer deduced from at all:

              int func();
              auto called = func;

            'called' used to be wrapped as an 'int', again with no warning. It
            is now ignored with warning 346.

2026-09-12: wsfulton
            A C++11 'auto' declaration declaring more than one variable now
            deduces the type from any of its declarators and not just from
            those whose initialiser is a name or a single literal:

              int get_int();
              auto first = get_int(), second = 1 + 2;

            C++ deduces one type for the whole declaration, so both variables
            are int. Both used to be ignored with warning 346, while writing
            the same declarators the other way round wrapped both.

2026-09-12: wsfulton
            A 'decltype(auto)' variable initialised by a string literal is now
            ignored with warning 346 instead of being wrapped:

              decltype(auto) text = "abc";

            A string literal is an lvalue of array type, so the variable is a
            reference to an array of characters and not the 'const char *' that
            ordinary 'auto' deduces. It used to be wrapped as the pointer, and
            the generated setter assigned to the reference and did not compile.

2026-09-12: wsfulton
            A function parameter is now in scope in a trailing return type, so
            a decltype there names the parameter rather than whatever else the
            name denotes outside the function:

              double value;
              auto shadow(int value) -> decltype(value);   // returns int
              auto square(float input) -> decltype(input); // returns float

            The first function used to be wrapped as returning double, with no
            warning, and the second reported warning 344 and generated a
            wrapper that did not compile.

2026-09-12: wsfulton
            Correct documentation inconsistencies in the warnings that SWIG
            emits. The list of warnings in the Warnings chapter of the manual
            has been checked against the messages in the source: incorrect
            message text has been corrected, warning numbers that were never
            documented have been added, and numbers that can no longer be
            issued have been removed.

            Two messages were themselves wrong and have been fixed. Warning
            385 reported "operator-- ignored" for a comma operator, and the
            D warning 710 contained a non-ASCII dash.

            Two messages were issued with an unrelated warning number. A
            missing Go typemap is now reported as warning 465 rather than
            507, and a value given to the doxygen:ignore feature is now
            reported as the new warning 567 rather than 206.

2026-09-11: wsfulton
            [Python] #3528 The .pyi stub file now declares the module scope functions that are
            declared inside a class, such as a friend function or a renamed constructor. These
            are wrapped at module scope but were only ever written to the proxy .py file, so a
            type checker reading the stub could not see them:

              struct Foo {
                %rename(MakeFoo) Foo(const Foo &);
                friend int value(const Foo &);
              };

            now declares both MakeFoo and value in the stub, after the Foo class body.

2026-09-06: wsfulton
            [Python] #3528 %feature("python:annotations", "c") no longer generates the PEP 484
            support code that only applies to %feature("python:annotations", "typing"). The
            _swig_property helper is neither generated nor used, so a member variable creates
            its property directly again:

              class V(object):
                  val: "float" = property(_example.V_val_get, _example.V_val_set)

            The .pyi stub file no longer declares thisown, __disown__ or the cvar globals
            holder with the typing.Any catch-all type in this mode either. C/C++ annotations
            are not type hints and are never type checked, so code that exists solely for a
            type checker has no purpose in them. A stub with no annotations at all, from -pyi
            used without -typehints, still declares all three.

2026-08-31: wsfulton
            [Python] #3528 A .pyi stub file is now generated for the low-level C/C++ module when
            -typehints or -pyi is used. Wrapping example.i now also generates _example.pyi
            alongside example.py, declaring the wrappers the module exports:

              def new_Foo(*args: "typing.Any", **kwargs: "typing.Any") -> "typing.Any":
                  ...

            The low-level module is a shared library, so a type checker cannot read it and
            reported the import of it in the generated .py file as an error. The wrappers are C
            functions, so what they take and return is annotated with the catch-all type, as are
            the constants, the cvar global variables holder and, with -builtin, the wrapped types.

            Everything the module exports is declared, so a wrapper name that is missing from the
            stub is reported rather than quietly accepted.

            The .pyi stub file generated by -pyi also declares the cvar holder, which it did not
            declare before even though the .py file assigns it.

2026-08-29: wsfulton
            [Python] #3528 std::unordered_set and std::unordered_multiset now have the add and
            discard methods that std::set and std::multiset already had. They are needed to
            register the unordered containers with the abstract base classes, as
            collections.abc.MutableSet declares both abstract:

              %pythonabc(std::unordered_set, "collections.abc.MutableSet");

2026-08-29: wsfulton
            [Python] #3528 The __contains__ method generated for std::set, std::multiset,
            std::map, std::multimap and the unordered forms of all four now returns False for
            an object that cannot be converted to the key type, instead of raising a TypeError,
            so that 'x in c' behaves as it does for the Python containers:

              "not an int" in IntSet([1])   # was a TypeError, now False

2026-08-29: wsfulton
            [Python] #3528 The pop method generated for the sequence containers now takes the
            optional index to remove, defaulting to the last element as before, so that
            std::vector, std::list and std::deque behave as a Python list does:

              v.pop(0)   # was a TypeError, now removes and returns the first element

            The last element is still removed without walking the container, so popping from a
            std::list is unchanged at O(1).

2026-08-28: wsfulton
            [Python] #3528 The std::basic_string methods returning a string by value, such as
            substr and __str__, are now annotated str instead of the catch-all typing.Any. A
            method returning a pointer or a reference, such as __add__, returns the proxy class
            rather than a str and is unchanged.

            Only the instantiations converted to and from a Python str are annotated as one.
            Any other instantiation is wrapped as a proxy object with none of the str methods
            on it, so it keeps the catch-all type.

2026-08-28: wsfulton
            [Python] #3528 The __str__, __repr__ and __format__ methods are annotated as
            returning str rather than typing.Optional[str] when they return a char *:

              struct Foo {
                const char *__str__() const { return "Foo"; }
              };

              def __str__(self) -> "str":

            A char * is annotated with typing.Optional as it can be a null pointer, which
            is None, but the Python data model requires these methods to return a string
            and a TypeError is raised if they do not. Any other method returning a char *
            is unchanged, as is one of these returning some other type, which is a genuine
            mismatch a type checker should report.

2026-08-27: wsfulton
            [Python] #3528 An overloaded function whose overloads do not all return the same type
            is now annotated typing.Any instead of the type returned by one of them:

              int    value(int i);
              double value(double d);

              def value(*args) -> "typing.Any":
                  return _example.value(*args)

            Which overload runs is not known until the function is called, so the annotation used
            previously was whichever overload happened to be last, which was not necessarily the
            one called. For example, std::set::erase was annotated as returning None although
            erase(key) returns the number of elements removed.

            The C/C++ annotations generated by

              %feature("python:annotations", "c")

            had the same defect, but there is no one C/C++ type to name, so nothing is annotated
            at all:

              def overloaded(*args):
                  return _example.overloaded(*args)

            The parameters of an overloaded function were already left unannotated. Overloads
            which do all return the same type are unchanged in both modes. The comparison is of
            the annotations rather than the C++ types, so overloads returning int and long still
            agree.

            The C/C++ annotations are not PEP 484 type hints and are not type checked, so they
            are no longer decorated with the _swig_dispatch helper either, nor is the **kwargs
            widening for type checkers added to them in the .pyi stub file.

2026-08-26: wsfulton
            [Python] #3528 Overloaded methods generated with -typehints are now decorated with
            a _swig_dispatch helper:

              class Derived(Base):
                  @_swig_dispatch
                  def f(self, *args) -> "None":
                      return _example.Derived_f(self, *args)

            An overloaded method is generated as a *args dispatcher, which cannot accept the
            keyword arguments that a named parameter list accepts and so is a narrower
            signature than any base class method it overrides, which type checkers reject. The
            decorator does nothing at runtime, but when type checking it replaces the parameter
            list with ..., which is compatible with any signature, keeping the return type.

            The same applies to any other method generated with a *args parameter list, such as
            when the compactdefaultargs feature is used.

            A .pyi stub file is never executed, so a dispatcher declared in one is widened with
            a **kwargs parameter instead of being decorated:

              class Derived(Base):
                  @staticmethod
                  def create(*args, **kwargs) -> "Widget":
                      ...

2026-08-25: wsfulton
            [Python] #3528 Annotated member variables are now created with a _swig_property helper
            instead of calling property directly:

              class V(object):
                  val: "float" = _swig_property(_example.V_val_get, _example.V_val_set)

            The variable annotation declares the type the property returns, but the value
            assigned is a property object, which type checkers reject. The helper returns
            typing.Any when typing.TYPE_CHECKING is set and is just an alias for property at
            runtime, so the generated code behaves exactly as before. Member variables that
            are not annotated, such as when -typehints is not used, are unchanged.

2026-08-24: wsfulton
            [Python] #3528 The proxy classes generated with -typehints now declare the 'this'
            attribute, which is added to each instance by the C code and so was not visible
            to type checkers analysing the __disown__ method:

              class Base(object):
                  if typing.TYPE_CHECKING:
                      this: "typing.Any"

            The declaration is also generated when -pyi is used, as the .py file still
            contains the __disown__ method that uses it. It is a variable annotation, so the
            python:annotations:novar feature turns it off along with all the others, keeping
            a novar build free of the PEP 526 syntax that requires Python 3.6.

            Bug fix so that the type wrapper classes are no longer generated into the .py
            file when -pyi is specified, as they were always present in the .pyi file. They
            only exist to give an annotation a named type to refer to and the annotations
            are all in the .pyi file when -pyi is used.

2026-08-23: wsfulton
            [Python] #3518 The pytyping typemaps added for the INPUT, INOUT and OUTPUT
            typemaps in typemaps.i are no longer all the same, as the typemaps do not all
            accept or return None. The pointer forms of INPUT and INOUT accept a null
            pointer, which is None, so they are now annotated with typing.Optional:

              %apply int *INPUT { int *value };
              void take_value(int *value);

            is now annotated:

              def take_value(value: "typing.Optional[int]") -> "None":

            INOUT returns what it was given, so its return type is annotated with
            typing.Optional as well:

              %apply int *INOUT { int *io };
              void modify_value(int *io);

            is now annotated:

              def modify_value(io: "typing.Optional[int]") -> "typing.Optional[int]":

            The reference forms, such as int &INPUT, are unchanged, as they reject a null
            pointer.

2026-08-23: wsfulton
            [JavaScript, Octave, Perl, Python, R, Ruby, Scilab, Tcl]
            Fix a crash when a null pointer is passed to a parameter using the INPUT or
            INOUT typemaps in typemaps.i for a reference, such as int &INPUT. A reference
            cannot be bound to a null pointer, but one was accepted and then dereferenced
            when making the call. It is now rejected with a null reference error, the same
            as for a plain SWIGTYPE & parameter and for the INPUT typemaps covering types
            such as std::string. For example:

              %apply int &INPUT { int &value };
              int get(int &value);

            passing the target language's null value, such as None in Python, to get now
            raises an error instead of crashing. The pointer typemaps, such as int *INPUT,
            are unchanged and continue to accept a null pointer.

2026-08-22: Nerixyz
            [Python] #2165 #3518 The INPUT, INOUT and OUTPUT typemaps in typemaps.i now
            carry pytyping typemaps, so a parameter using them is annotated with the type
            it really takes or returns rather than the catch-all typing.Any:

              %apply double *OUTPUT { double *out };
              void my_func(double *out);

            is now annotated:

              def my_func() -> "float":

2026-08-16: wsfulton
            [Python] #3532 New python:annotations:catchall feature supplies the PEP 484
            annotation for everything a function returns when SWIG cannot work it out
            and would otherwise use the catch-all type, typing.Any. This is the case
            when the argout typemaps disagree on the container and warning 478 would
            be issued:

              %feature("python:annotations:catchall") tuple_then_list
                "typing.List[typing.Union[typing.Tuple[bool, int], int]]"

            tuple_then_list is annotated with the type given rather than typing.Any and
            warning 478 is not issued for it, as the feature supplies what the function
            returns. The feature supplies a PEP 484 type, so it does nothing for the
            C/C++ annotation types and warning 478 is still issued in that mode.

2026-08-16: wsfulton
            [Lua] The $isvoid special variable is now expanded in director methods, so a
            directorin, directorout or directorargout typemap can use it. It was
            previously left as $isvoid in the generated C++ code, which did not compile.

2026-08-16: wsfulton
            An invalid numinputs value in an in typemap is now an error. The value was
            previously parsed leniently, taking any leading digits and ignoring the rest,
            so numinputs="abc" was read as 0 and quietly dropped the parameter from the
            wrapped function, while numinputs="1x" was read as 1. The value must now be
            written as a non-negative integer:

              %typemap(in, numinputs="abc") int * ""

            now reports:

              Error: Invalid numinputs value 'abc' in the in typemap. It must be a
              non-negative integer.

2026-08-16: wsfulton
            Add the container attribute to the argout typemap, saying what the typemap
            appends into when more than one value is returned. It defaults to "list",
            what SWIG_AppendOutput builds, and "tuple" is also supported for typemaps
            using a replacement for SWIG_AppendOutput that builds a fixed size sequence.
            On a typemap also using overwrite=1 it names the container the typemap
            assigns to $result, which holds a single value too.

            New warning 478 is issued when the argout typemaps matched for one function
            do not all build the same container, as they all append into the same
            $result. The type of the values returned cannot then be worked out and a
            catch-all type, such as typing.Any for Python, is used for the return type.

2026-08-16: wsfulton
            #3469 #684 Add the numoutputs and overwrite attributes to the argout
            typemap, so that a typemap can say what it returns rather than SWIG
            assuming every argout typemap appends exactly one value:

              // returns nothing, just turns an error code into an exception
              %typemap(argout, numoutputs=0) int *ERRCHECK { ... }

              // replaces $result rather than appending to it
              %typemap(argout, overwrite=1) (void *buffer, size_t len) { ... }

            numoutputs defaults to 1, so existing argout typemaps are unaffected.
            The attributes are ignored by target languages that do not describe the
            return type of a wrapped function.

2026-08-16: wsfulton
            #3084 Add the numoutputs attribute to the out typemap. An out typemap
            declaring numoutputs=0 does not return the function return value, so the
            only values returned are those the argout typemaps append. This suits a C
            function that signals errors through its return value:

              %typemap(out, numoutputs=0) MyErr ""

              %typemap(ret) MyErr %{
                if (result != 0) {
                  PyErr_Format(PyExc_RuntimeError, "error code %d", (int)result);
                  SWIG_fail;
                }
              %}

              %apply int *OUTPUT { int *out };

              MyErr get_value(int *out);

            get_value now returns just the value of out, rather than a list holding the
            suppressed return value followed by out. Previously this needed an out
            typemap pushing a placeholder plus a ret typemap unpicking it again.

            A new $isvoidresult special variable provides the $isvoid functionality
            extended to cover this case. It expands to 1 for a void return, just as
            $isvoid does, and additionally for a non void return that an out typemap
            has suppressed with numoutputs=0. In other words it asks whether the wrapped
            function returns a value to the target language, rather than whether the C++
            return type is void, which is what the argout typemaps need to know when
            deciding whether there is anything to append to.

            $isvoid is unchanged and continues to describe the C++ return type, so
            typemaps relying on that meaning are unaffected. The SWIG_AppendOutput macro
            now uses $isvoidresult in its place.

2026-08-16: wsfulton
            [Python] Stop generating a return annotation on __init__ when a constructor
            has a parameter using an argout typemap. Python requires __init__ to return
            None, and constructors without such a parameter never had one. For example:

              %typemap(in, numinputs=0) short *flag (short temp) { $1 = &temp; }
              %typemap(argout) short *flag { (void)*$1; }
              %typemap(pytyping) short *flag "None"

              struct Counter {
                Counter(int v, short *flag);
              };

            previously generated a return annotation built from the argout typemaps:

              def __init__(self, v: "int") -> "typing.List[typing.Union[typing.Any, None]]":

            and now generates:

              def __init__(self, v: "int"):

2026-08-14: wsfulton
            [Javascript, PHP] Warning 314 no longer repeats the new name when
            renaming a Javascript keyword or the PHP 'readonly' keyword:

              Warning 314: 'this' is a javascript keyword, renaming to '_this'

            The ", renaming to '_this'" clause used to be shown twice.

2026-08-14: wsfulton
            [Octave] A parameter defaulting to a null pointer is now shown as
            the empty matrix in the documentation generated by the autodoc
            feature, instead of the Python literal None:

              @deftypefn {Loadable Function} @var{retval} = f (@var{p} = [])

            The empty matrix is what the Octave wrappers accept for, and return
            as, a null pointer.

2026-08-13: wsfulton
            Parse a C++17 structured binding, which was previously a syntax
            error at namespace or class scope:

              auto [a, b] = pt;
              auto& [c, d] = pt;
              const auto& [e, f] = pt;
              auto&& [g, h] = Pt{1, 2};
              static auto [i, j] = pt;

            Giving each name a type means knowing how the initialiser
            decomposes, which SWIG does not work out, so the declaration is
            ignored with the new warning 349. Parsing then carries on, where
            before the syntax error stopped the rest of the file being parsed.

2026-08-13: wsfulton
            Add support for the C++14 'decltype(auto)' placeholder, which was a
            syntax error in every position it is allowed in:

              decltype(auto) v = g;
              decltype(auto) f() { return g; }
              auto t() -> decltype(auto);
              struct S { decltype(auto) m() { return 1; } };
              struct T { operator decltype(auto)() { return 1; } };

            The error could not be recovered from, so the rest of the file was
            not parsed.

            A 'decltype(auto)' variable is deduced from its initialiser, keeping
            the references and top level cv-qualifiers that 'auto' drops, so
            'decltype(auto) r = ref;' is wrapped as a reference. Used as a return
            type it has to be deduced from the function body, which SWIG does not
            analyse, so the function is ignored with warning 345 as before.

            Warning 345 no longer advises adding a trailing return type when
            there could not be one that helps, that is when the trailing return
            type is itself a placeholder, or when the declaration is a conversion
            function, which cannot have one at all.

2026-08-13: wsfulton
            Accept an undecorated C++20 'auto' parameter pack, which was
            previously a syntax error where the decorated spellings such as
            'auto&&...' and 'auto*...' were accepted:

              int sum(auto... args) { return (args + ... + 0); }
              template<auto... Ns> struct T {};

2026-08-12: wsfulton
            Accept a defaulted or deleted function that has an 'auto' return
            type and a cv-qualifier, noexcept-specifier or ref-qualifier, such
            as the canonical C++20 defaulted three-way comparison operator,
            which was previously a syntax error:

              struct S {
                int v;
                auto operator<=>(const S&) const = default;
              };

            SWIG cannot deduce the return type, so the operator is ignored with
            warning 345.

2026-08-12: wsfulton
            Accept a trailing return type in a C++11 alias declaration and alias
            template, which was previously a syntax error even though the
            equivalent typedef was accepted:

              using alt_callback_t = auto (*)(int) -> int;
              using alt_function_t = auto (int) -> int;
              template<typename T> using alt_fn_t = auto (*)(T) -> T;

2026-08-12: wsfulton
            Add support for the C++14 conversion function with a deduced return
            type, which was previously a syntax error:

              struct S {
                operator auto() const { return 42; }
              };

            SWIG cannot deduce the type, so the conversion function is ignored
            with warning 345, just like any other function with an 'auto' return
            type and no trailing return type.

2026-08-12: wsfulton
            Accept any type as the explicit trailing return type of a lambda,
            including the C++14 'auto' placeholder. These were previously a
            syntax error:

              auto negate_value = [](int x) -> auto { return -x; };
              auto address = [](int &x) -> int * { return &x; };

2026-08-12: wsfulton
            Accept a C++20 trailing requires-clause on a function declared with
            a trailing return type, which was previously a syntax error:

              template<typename T> auto f(T t) -> T requires Numeric<T> { return t; }

2026-08-12: wsfulton
            Accept any type as the C++11 trailing return type of a function.
            Pointers, references to a primitive type, array references and
            function pointers were previously a syntax error:

              auto count() -> const int&;
              auto data() -> int *;
              auto values() -> int (&)[3];
              auto callback() -> int (*)(int);

            A pointer to member has to be written with parentheses, that is
            "auto member() -> int (S::*);". The unparenthesised
            "auto member() -> int S::*;" that C++ also accepts remains a syntax
            error.

2026-08-12: wsfulton
            Add support for the C++23 explicit object parameter ("deducing
            this"), where a member function declares its first parameter with
            the 'this' specifier:

              struct S {
                int m(this S& self);
                auto n(this auto&& self) -> int;
              };

            Both are now wrapped as member functions taking no arguments - the
            explicit object parameter is how the object itself is passed, so it
            is not one of the function's arguments. The deduced spelling was
            previously wrapped as a method taking one argument and the
            non-deduced spelling was a syntax error.

            A lambda can have an explicit object parameter too, which is how a
            recursive lambda is written, and is parsed the same way. A lambda is
            still wrapped as an opaque object.

            A member function with an explicit object parameter cannot be
            static or virtual and cannot be declared with a cv-qualifier or a
            ref-qualifier, and an explicit object parameter is not valid on a
            function that is not a member function. Each of these is now an
            error.

2026-08-12: wsfulton
            Fix an 'auto' parameter pack in an abbreviated function template
            inventing a template parameter that was not a parameter pack, which
            made every %template instantiation of such a function fail with
            "No matching function template":

              int sum(const auto&... args) { return (args + ... + 0); }

              %template(sum_ii)  sum<int, int>;
              %template(sum_iii) sum<int, int, int>;

            The invented parameter is now a template parameter pack, exactly as
            it is for the equivalent 'template<typename... T>' spelling, so the
            number of types given to %template is the number of parameters the
            wrapped function takes - sum_ii takes two and sum_iii takes three.

2026-08-12: wsfulton
            Say what got in the way of deducing an 'auto' type in warnings 345
            and 346, which previously read the same whether the initialiser was
            one SWIG could not deduce a type from or in a form SWIG does not
            support at all:

              Warning 346: Unable to deduce auto type for variable 'v1'
              from initialiser 'fn(1)' (ignored).

              Warning 346: Unable to deduce auto type for variable 'v2'
              from an unsupported initialiser (ignored).

              Warning 345: Unable to deduce auto return type for 'f()'
              without a trailing return type (ignored).

2026-08-12: wsfulton
            Deduce the type of a named cast, both for an 'auto' variable and for
            decltype:

              auto d = static_cast<double>(i);  // double

            A cast to a type SWIG has no built in type code for, such as a cast
            to a class or to a reference, remains undeducible.

            The type a named cast casts to is now used anywhere else the type of
            a value expression matters. A %constant with no explicit type was
            previously always made an int:

              %constant CD = static_cast<double>(3);

            is a double in the target language instead of an int, and an
            enumerator initialised with a cast to char or bool is typed the same
            way as one initialised with a character or boolean literal.

            *** POTENTIAL INCOMPATIBILITY ***
            An enumerator initialised with a cast to a non-integral type, such
            as 'enum E { A = static_cast<double>(1) };', is now the error
            "Type error. Expecting an integral type" rather than being silently
            treated as an int. Such an enumerator is not valid C++ either.

2026-08-12: wsfulton
            Deduce the type of an 'auto' variable initialised with a braced
            initialiser instead of discarding the initialiser:

              auto v{42};   // int

            The value was previously dropped and the variable ignored with
            warning 346. A braced initialiser holding a single element deduces
            the type of that element, which is the C++17 rule and also what
            compilers implementing N3922 do in C++11 and C++14 mode. An empty or
            multiple element braced initialiser is still undeducible, and is not
            valid C++ for an 'auto' variable anyway.

2026-08-12: wsfulton
            Fix an 'auto' declaration that declares more than one variable
            dropping all but the first declarator:

              auto x = 1, y = 2;

            Previously only 'x' reached the parse tree, and it was ignored with
            warning 346 as the initialiser never reached the deduction. Both
            variables are now wrapped. C++ requires every declarator in the list
            to deduce to the same type, so a declarator SWIG cannot deduce a type
            from takes the type deduced for the others, and each declarator keeps
            its own decoration:

              int g = 42;
              int fn(int x);
              auto v = 1, *p = &g;    // int v and int *p
              auto u = fn(1), w = 2;  // both int, deduced from '2'

            Only a declaration no declarator at all deduces a type from is
            dropped, and warning 346 then names each variable's own initialiser
            rather than blaming one declarator for another's. Two declarators
            deducing different types is not valid C++ and is reported by the new
            warning 348, with each variable still wrapped with the type its own
            initialiser deduced:

              Warning 348: Inconsistent auto type deduction for variable 'y':
              initialiser '2.5' deduces 'double', not 'int'.

2026-08-12: wsfulton
            Deduce the type of an 'auto' variable from the declaration its
            initialiser refers to. The declarator of that declaration is now part
            of the deduced type, and the address of something in scope deduces to
            a pointer to it, so the variables below are wrapped instead of being
            ignored with warning 346:

              int g = 42;
              int *pg = &g;
              int fn(int x);
              auto v1 = pg;     // int *
              auto* v2 = &g;    // int *
              auto** v3 = &pg;  // int **
              auto* v4 = &fn;   // int (*)(int)

            The top level cv-qualifiers of the initialiser are dropped unless the
            variable is a reference, as C++ requires:

              const int cg = 42;
              auto v5 = cg;   // int
              auto& r1 = cg;  // const int &

            'decltype' deduces types the same way, so 'decltype(pg)' is now
            'int *' rather than 'int'.

2026-08-11: wsfulton
            Fix syntax error parsing an 'auto' variable declared with a
            declarator. The placeholder can now carry a reference, rvalue
            reference, pointer or cv-qualifier, matching what an 'auto' function
            parameter has always accepted:

              int g = 42;
              int *pg = &g;
              auto& r1 = g;
              const auto& r2 = g;
              auto&& r3 = 42;
              auto* p1 = &g;
              auto* const p2 = &g;
              auto** p3 = &pg;
              Numeric auto& r4 = g;

            The decoration is kept on the declared variable, so 'r2' above wraps
            as a read only int reference. Where SWIG cannot deduce the
            placeholder from the initialiser, the variable is still ignored after
            issuing warning 346, as it was before for an undeducible initialiser
            without a declarator.

            The same declarator is now also accepted on a lambda:

              auto&& lambda = [](int x) { return x; };

2026-08-11: wsfulton
            Fix syntax error parsing a C++20 concept whose body is an ordinary
            expression rather than a chain of concept names:

              template<typename T> concept Anything = true;
              template<typename T> concept NonEmpty = sizeof(T) > 0;

            Previously only a parenthesised expression was accepted, so the
            equivalent '= (true)' worked while '= true' did not.

2026-08-11: wsfulton
            Add support for the C++26 reason on a deleted function:

              void f() = delete("use g() instead");

            The reason is parsed and discarded; the function is treated exactly
            like one declared with a plain '= delete'.

2026-08-11: wsfulton
            Fix syntax error parsing a cv-qualified 'auto' placeholder. Both
            orderings of the qualifier are now accepted everywhere a placeholder
            is allowed - function return types, variables, function parameters and
            lambdas - including with a C++20 type-constraint:

              const auto& cref();
              auto const& cref2();
              const Numeric auto& cref3();
              static const auto cz = 2;
              const auto lambda = [](int x) { return x; };
              int fn(auto const& x);

            Only the 'const auto& x' ordering on a function parameter was accepted
            before, and only without a qualifier anywhere else.

            For a variable the qualifier is kept on the deduced type, so 'cz' above
            wraps as a read only int. For a function SWIG still cannot deduce the
            return type, so it is ignored after issuing warning 345; use %ignore
            plus a declaration with an explicit return type to wrap it.

            A trailing return type requires the placeholder on its own, so a
            qualifier combined with one is now reported as an error rather than
            being accepted:

              const auto& fn(int x) -> int;   // Error, not valid C++

2026-08-08: wsfulton
            [Python] The container attribute on the argout typemap selects the PEP 484
            annotation used for multiple returned values, typing.List[typing.Union[...]]
            for a list and typing.Tuple[...] for a tuple:

              %typemap(argout, container="tuple") short *OutTuple {
                $result = MyTupleAppend($result, PyLong_FromLong(*$1), $isvoidresult);
              }
              %typemap(pytyping) short *OutTuple "int"

              void two_values(short *OutTuple, short *OutTuple2);

            is annotated -> "typing.Tuple[int, int]".

2026-08-08: wsfulton
            [Python] #3469 #684 PEP 484 return annotations now take the numoutputs
            and overwrite argout typemap attributes into account. An argout typemap
            declaring numoutputs=0 no longer contributes a type to the annotation and
            one declaring overwrite=1 discards the types collected before it, so:

              %typemap(argout, numoutputs=0) short *OutCheck { ... }
              %typemap(pytyping) short *OutCheck "None"

              bool check_only(bool arg, short *OutCheck);

            is now annotated -> "bool" rather than
            -> "typing.List[typing.Union[bool, None]]".

